models/metabolism.py + sbml_model.py: SBML→ODE simulation, Chassagnole 2002 verified

OpenCell now consumes curated BioModels SBML directly and integrates it
with the existing SciPy solver.  First sub-model anchored on real biology,
agreeing to ~1e-6 relative with libroadrunner across every species.

Engine (opencell/models/sbml_model.py):
- libsbml parses the SBML; sympy.lambdify compiles every <kineticLaw> and
  <assignmentRule> MathML formula to a NumPy callable.
- Identifiers are pre-bound to sympy.Symbol via local_dict so common SBML
  names (S, E, I, N, O, Q) are not silently shadowed by sympy singletons.
- Loud failure on unsupported features: <event>, <functionDefinition>,
  <rateRule>/<algebraicRule>, hasOnlySubstanceUnits=true.
- Provenance: sbml_path + SHA-256 of the bytes + level/version + topology.

Wrapper (opencell/models/metabolism.py):
- MetabolismModel.load() pins BIOMD0000000051 (E. coli central carbon).
- Records BioModels ID + paper DOI 10.1002/bit.10288 + PMID 17590932 in
  provenance() so simulation outputs trace back to the eutils-verified paper.

Validation (tests/integration/test_metabolism_chassagnole.py):
- libroadrunner is the de facto SBML simulator (Tellurium ships it).
- 18 species, 7 sample times in 0..60s, rtol=1e-3 atol=1e-6 — all pass.
- Initial conditions match RR exactly; no negative concentrations.

Demo (scripts/run_chassagnole.py):
- Runs 60s of glycolytic dynamics, writes artifacts/chassagnole_60s.png,
  emits full provenance JSON to stdout.

Tests: 357 → 378 passing (+21), no regressions, full suite 4m16s.

Dependencies: added sympy>=1.13 to runtime, libroadrunner>=2.7 to oracle.

